
# THE COPY OF THIS FILE MUST BE MODE 0600. This template carries no secrets and is
# committed world-readable, which is right for a template and wrong for the .env made from
# it: that one holds the PostgreSQL password, the Kafka cluster-superuser credential, the
# event publisher's credential and the sample subscriber's, and "cp" reproduces this file's
# 0644 so every account on the host can read all four.
#
# './stack.sh --init' handles it: it creates .env with mode 0600 as the file comes into
# existence, repairs the mode of an .env that already exists BEFORE writing anything into
# it, generates every secret above, and fills in only the keys that are still missing - so
# it is safe to re-run against an environment already in service, and it is how an .env
# written before Kafka existed gains its Kafka keys.
#
# If you copy this file by hand instead, run 'install -m 600 .env.example .env' rather than
# 'cp', and set every empty credential yourself.

# Which docker-compose file to use by default
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yaml

# Command line for docker-compose.
# You may need to modify it, as some Docker confugurations use "docker compose" configuration.
#COMPOSE_CL="docker compose"
COMPOSE_CL="docker-compose"

# docker-compose stack name
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=blnk

# Blnk project name
BLNK_PROJECT_NAME=Blnk

# Timezone
TZ=Etc/UTC

# Server details
BLNK_SERVER_SSL_DOMAIN=
BLNK_SERVER_SSL_EMAIL=
BLNK_SERVER_SSL=
BLNK_SERVER_PORT=

# DNS details
BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS=
BLNK_REDIS_DNS=

# Postgres credentials
#POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:latest
POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:alpine3.19 #alphine version is smaller in size
POSTGRES_CONTAINER=pg_server
POSTGRES_OUTER_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_INNER_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD={POSTGRES_PASSWORD}
POSTGRES_DB=blnk
POSTGRES_SCHEMA=blnk

# Kafka event streaming
# Read by Blnk itself. Every key in this block mirrors a field of config.KafkaConfig,
# in declaration order, so the block and the struct can be diffed by eye. Blnk reads
# them from its process environment, or from the matching keys under "kafka" in
# blnk.json.
#
# FOUR OF THEM ARE THE DEPLOYMENT CONTRACT: KAFKA_BROKERS, KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX,
# KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_USER and KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_SECRET. Together with the three RELAY_*
# keys further down and WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_SUNSET_DATE they are the eight variables
# requirement R-10 fixes, and their names, defaults and meanings do not change.
#
# Everything else in this block is a SUPPLEMENTARY setting - Blnk's own, revisable, and
# never required for a working deployment because each has a safe default or a
# documented fallback. Each is labelled where it appears, with the reason it exists:
# KAFKA_SASL_USER/SECRET for producer least privilege, KAFKA_SASL_SECRET_FILE as the
# no-print delivery channel for a generated one, KAFKA_TLS_* for transport security,
# KAFKA_INSECURE_LOCAL_DEV as the explicit local-dev acknowledgement,
# KAFKA_MIN_PARTITIONS/KAFKA_REPLICATION_FACTOR for topic geometry, and
# KAFKA_ALLOW_PARTITION_GROWTH as consent for a reordering-unsafe operation. The
# distinction is recorded here rather than left to a document because the two have
# different standing, and confusing them is how a contract quietly grows.
#
# The missing BLNK_ prefix is deliberate - do not "correct" it. envconfig builds each
# field's primary key by accumulating the prefix through every enclosing struct and
# consults the raw tag literal only as an alternate key, so for this nested block the
# prefixed primary envconfig derives is BLNK_KAFKA_KAFKA_BROKERS.
#
# The ordinary BLNK_-prefixed alias of every key in this block - BLNK_KAFKA_BROKERS,
# BLNK_KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX and so on - ALSO resolves, and wins when both forms are set.
# It is applied explicitly by applyPrefixedEnvAliases in config/config.go, because
# envconfig alone reads it under neither key: a deployment that set it used to get
# default behaviour with no error to explain why nothing was published. Either
# spelling below is therefore supported; the bare one is the mandated contract.
#
# Leaving KAFKA_BROKERS empty is a legitimate steady state, not an error: with no
# brokers configured the event publisher resolves to its no-op implementation and
# Blnk starts, serves and processes transactions exactly as it did before Kafka
# existed. Set it only when you want events published. Comma-separated; whitespace
# around each entry is trimmed and empty entries are dropped. Use the broker's
# service name inside the compose network, or localhost:9092 for a host-run binary.
#
# IN THE COMPOSE STACK THIS IS ONLY HALF OF OPTING IN. The kafka and kafka-init
# services sit behind the "kafka" Compose profile, so setting this key alone leaves
# the relay retrying against a broker nobody started. Set COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka near
# the bottom of this file as well, or pass --profile kafka.
#KAFKA_BROKERS=kafka:9092
KAFKA_BROKERS=

# The SUBSCRIBER-FACING bootstrap list: the externally advertised broker addresses
# POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials reports to a subscriber. It is a DIFFERENT
# LIST from KAFKA_BROKERS above, not an alias for it.
#
# KAFKA_BROKERS is what Blnk itself dials, and inside a deployment that is an internal
# address - "kafka:9092" here, a ClusterIP or headless Service in Kubernetes. Neither
# resolves for a subscriber outside the deployment, and Kafka compounds it: a broker
# answers every client with the ADVERTISED address of the listener the connection
# arrived on, so even a reachable bootstrap hands back internal names for the partition
# leaders. Only an operator knows the externally advertised list.
#
# LEAVING IT EMPTY REFUSES ISSUANCE with 503 SUBSCRIBER_BROKERS_NOT_CONFIGURED; it does
# NOT fall back to KAFKA_BROKERS. The fallback would return 200 carrying an endpoint the
# subscriber cannot dial, so the failure would surface as an unexplained connection
# timeout in the subscriber's own logs days later - and would publish Blnk's internal
# topology to an external party on the way. The refusal is warned about at start-up, so
# an operator learns of it from the boot log rather than from a 503 mid-onboarding.
#
# The local value is the broker's EXTERNAL listener, which docker-compose.yaml publishes
# on the host and advertises as ${KAFKA_OUTER_ADVERTISED_HOST:-localhost}:9092. A
# deployment whose subscribers really do run inside it sets this to the same value as
# KAFKA_BROKERS, which makes that claim explicit in one line.
KAFKA_SUBSCRIBER_BROKERS=localhost:9092

# The prefix every topic name is derived from. Default: blnk, which yields the five
# category topics blnk.transactions, blnk.balances, blnk.identities, blnk.ledgers and
# blnk.system, plus their blnk.<category>.dlt dead-letter siblings - ten owned topics
# in total. The first four are grantable to subscribers; blnk.system carries
# system.error and every event type the mapping table does not recognise, so it is
# internal and is never granted, and no dead-letter topic is ever grantable.
KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX=blnk


# SASL/SCRAM principal the STEADY-STATE EVENT PUBLISHER authenticates as - the
# identity the server and worker processes use to write events. It needs only Write
# and Describe on the topics Blnk owns, and it must NOT be the administrative
# principal below.
#
# REQUIRED once KAFKA_BROKERS is non-empty. This used to be advisory: leaving it empty
# made the publisher fall back to the administrative credential with a warning. The
# default was backwards, because a deployment reaches that fallback by leaving two
# variables unset - the state every deployment starts in - so a warning was the only
# thing standing between an ordinary rollout and publishing every ledger event as the
# principal that can create topics, mint SCRAM credentials and rewrite ACLs. A warning
# also changes nothing about what a leaked credential can then do.
#
# The publisher now REFUSES to start in that state and names these two variables. See
# KAFKA_ALLOW_ADMIN_PRODUCER below for the deliberate, temporary escape hatch.
#
# scripts/kafka-provision.sh creates this principal and its Write/Describe grants for
# the local stack, from KAFKA_PRODUCER_USER / KAFKA_PRODUCER_SECRET below; the compose
# services fall back to those two names so one .env entry serves both provisioning and
# runtime. stack.sh --init generates them.
#KAFKA_SASL_USER=blnk-producer
KAFKA_SASL_USER=

# Never commit a real secret here; see the note on KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_SECRET below,
# which applies verbatim.
#
# In the compose stack you normally leave this and KAFKA_SASL_USER above EMPTY and set
# the KAFKA_PRODUCER_* pair below instead: the server and worker default these two
# from those two, so one generated value both mints the principal on the broker and is
# presented by the publishing processes, and the two cannot drift. Set these names
# explicitly only to point at a principal provisioned outside this stack.
KAFKA_SASL_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local-stack principal provisioning
#
# The two blocks below are read by scripts/kafka-provision.sh, not by Blnk. They say
# which principals the local broker should hold; the KAFKA_SASL_* keys above say which
# one Blnk presents.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The STEADY-STATE PRODUCER principal scripts/kafka-provision.sh creates, and the pair
# the compose server and worker default KAFKA_SASL_USER / KAFKA_SASL_SECRET from.
#
# Its grant is Write and Describe on every Blnk-owned topic — each category topic and
# each .dlt sibling — and nothing else: no Read, no consumer group, no cluster
# operation. The dead-letter topics are included because dead-lettering is
# a publish: an event that exhausts its retry budget is written to <topic>.dlt by the
# same process on the same connection.
#
# ./stack.sh --init generates the secret into a mode-0600 .env. Left empty, the
# principal is SKIPPED rather than generated with a printed password — a generated
# password has to be delivered somewhere, and kafka-init's stdout is a container log
# that would keep it indefinitely.
KAFKA_PRODUCER_USER=blnk-producer
KAFKA_PRODUCER_SECRET=

# The SAMPLE SUBSCRIBER principal, a local-development convenience: nothing in Blnk
# needs it, and with the secret empty it is skipped and says so.
#
# Its grant is Read and Describe on the GRANTABLE category topics only — never the
# internal blnk.system topic, and never a .dlt sibling — plus Read on consumer groups
# under its own prefix. That is the same allowlist model.SubscriberGrantableTopics
# enforces for a real subscriber, so a grant this script will not make is one
# POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials would also refuse.
#
# KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_TOPICS NARROWS that grant to a comma-separated subset. It
# cannot widen it: every entry is checked against the same allowlist.
#
# Real subscriber credentials are never minted by a script. They are issued by
# POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials, which returns the secret once and persists
# only a non-reversible reference to it.
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_USER=blnk-sample-subscriber
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET=
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_GROUP_PREFIX=blnk-sample-subscriber
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_TOPICS=

# LAST-RESORT COMPATIBILITY SWITCH. Leave it false.
#
# Setting it true permits the event publisher to authenticate with the ADMINISTRATIVE
# credentials when no producer principal is configured. It exists for exactly one
# case: an existing single-credential deployment that must keep publishing while it
# provisions a producer principal, rather than stop dead on an upgrade. Every
# publisher construction that takes this path logs a security warning naming it.
#
# Running on it means the process handling the highest request volume in the
# deployment holds unconditional authority over the cluster's access model. Provision
# the producer principal and clear this.
KAFKA_ALLOW_ADMIN_PRODUCER=false

# SASL/SCRAM administrative principal, used for topic assurance, subscriber SCRAM
# credential provisioning, ACL grants and revocation, and the offset reads behind the
# consumer-lag metrics. Empty by default and never defaulted in code, because it is a
# credential: an unconfigured admin principal simply means no provisioning is
# attempted. The local stack bootstraps "admin".
#
# GIVE IT TO THE SERVER ONLY. cmd/server.go is the one process that builds a Kafka
# admin client; the worker publishes and consumes asynq queues and never does, so the
# compose worker service deliberately does not receive this pair. Putting the
# strongest identity in the cluster into a second process buys nothing it can use and
# doubles the surface it can be taken from. Publishing is KAFKA_SASL_USER's job.
#KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_USER=admin
KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_USER=

# Never commit a real secret here. This ships empty rather than as a brace
# placeholder like the POSTGRES_PASSWORD one above, and deliberately so: stack.sh
# --init substitutes that one name and nothing else, so any other placeholder would
# survive into .env as a literal password. Note that its sed is global, which is why
# no comment in this file spells a placeholder out.
#
# './stack.sh --init' fills this key in anyway, through a different route that reads
# the empty assignment rather than a placeholder - and it does so for an .env that
# already exists as well as for one it creates, so an environment written before
# Kafka existed gains the key by re-running it. For anything else, inject the value
# from your secret store, or set it by hand for local work - together with the
# username above, never on its own.
KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_SECRET=

# Transport security. BOTH Kafka clients - the event publisher and the administrative
# client - refuse to dial unless one of the two switches below is set, and shipping
# with neither set is deliberate: a deployment must CHOOSE, in writing, between
# verified TLS and an explicitly acknowledged plaintext connection. Neither can be
# arrived at by leaving a variable unset.
#
# Ledger events carry financial amounts and identity events carry names, email
# addresses, phone numbers, addresses and dates of birth, so an unencrypted broker
# connection exposes exactly the data this system exists to protect. SASL/SCRAM does
# not help here: it authenticates without a plaintext password crossing the wire, but
# it does not encrypt the messages that follow.
#
# PRODUCTION: set KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=true and point KAFKA_TLS_CA_FILE at the PEM
# bundle that signed the brokers' certificates. Add KAFKA_TLS_CERT_FILE and
# KAFKA_TLS_KEY_FILE together for mutual TLS. Set KAFKA_TLS_SERVER_NAME when brokers
# are reached through an address that does not match their advertised name.
#KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=true
#KAFKA_TLS_CA_FILE=/etc/blnk/kafka/ca.pem
#KAFKA_TLS_CERT_FILE=/etc/blnk/kafka/client.pem
#KAFKA_TLS_KEY_FILE=/etc/blnk/kafka/client-key.pem
#KAFKA_TLS_SERVER_NAME=kafka.internal.example.com
KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=false

# Last resort only, and warned about on every configuration load: TLS still encrypts
# but no longer AUTHENTICATES, so an interposed broker is indistinguishable from the
# real one. Configure KAFKA_TLS_CA_FILE instead.
KAFKA_TLS_INSECURE_SKIP_VERIFY=false

# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Permits an unencrypted connection, which is what the local
# single-broker KRaft stack in docker-compose listens for (SASL_PLAINTEXT). It is the
# only reason plaintext is reachable at all, and it is warned about on every
# configuration load so its presence in a real deployment cannot go unnoticed.
KAFKA_INSECURE_LOCAL_DEV=true

# Permits the topic-assurance pass to raise the partition count of a topic that
# ALREADY HOLDS MESSAGES. Leave it false.
#
# Growing a live topic re-maps keys to partitions - a key hashed into partition 2 of
# six lands somewhere else out of twelve - so one aggregate's history is split across
# two partitions and its events can be consumed out of order. That breaks the
# per-aggregate ordering guarantee irreversibly for every key already written.
# Assurance therefore refuses and reports instead, unless an operator has planned the
# migration and set this. An EMPTY topic is always grown: with no records written
# there is no mapping to preserve.
KAFKA_ALLOW_PARTITION_GROWTH=false


# Minimum partitions per topic; 6 is the required minimum. Topics are grown to this
# count when they already exist and are never shrunk. Messages are keyed by ledger
# ID, so ordering is per aggregate regardless of the count - more partitions buy
# parallelism, they do not weaken the ordering guarantee.
KAFKA_MIN_PARTITIONS=6

# 1 locally, 3 in production. A single-broker KRaft cluster cannot satisfy a
# replication factor of 3 - topic creation fails outright - so the local stack must
# say 1. The shipped code default is 3 for production durability, and because
# defaults only fill unset values the explicit 1 below always wins.
KAFKA_REPLICATION_FACTOR=1

# Webhook deprecation
# The dual-delivery window: Kafka publishing and legacy HTTP webhook delivery run
# concurrently from the same outbox rows for exactly 30 days, which is what guarantees
# both transports carry an identical payload. From the sunset instant onwards Kafka is
# the only transport and the deprecated webhook management routes answer 410 Gone.
# Both behaviours read one decision point, event_sunset.go, so they can never disagree
# about the date.
#
# THERE IS ONE SETTING, and it is the sunset. The window's opening instant is DERIVED
# as sunset minus 30 days and has no variable of its own, so "exactly 30 days" is
# arithmetic rather than something you have to get right. An earlier revision of this
# file also accepted WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_START_DATE; that name is retired and is now
# IGNORED if set, in both its bare and BLNK_-prefixed forms.
#
# A MALFORMED VALUE IS FATAL. Stating a retirement instant and mis-typing it would
# otherwise resolve silently to "the sunset has not passed", so one typo would keep the
# deprecated HTTP transport running indefinitely with nothing failing anywhere.
#
# LEAVING IT EMPTY IS A WARNING, not an error, with or without KAFKA_BROKERS set.
# Nothing has been mis-stated in that case - you have not chosen a retirement date yet -
# and adding KAFKA_BROKERS must not be able to take a deployment down. Dual delivery
# then continues, which is the pre-existing behaviour and is safe for every subscriber.
#
# THIS KEY SHIPS EMPTY, and that is a decision rather than an omission: a date committed
# in a template is in the past shortly after it is written, and a window that has already
# closed is worse than an absent one because it silently ends dual delivery. './stack.sh
# --init' does not fill it in either, for the same reason - an initialised stack is
# brought up and torn down repeatedly, so any instant it chose would be arbitrary.
#
# SET IT TO A DATE 30 DAYS FROM YOUR OWN CUTOVER, computed when you deploy. No example
# value is given on purpose: a literal date in a template is wrong the moment it is
# copied, and a sunset already in the past switches the legacy transport off
# immediately - which for a deployment mid-migration means subscribers that have not
# moved yet stop receiving anything. Generate it instead, for example:
#
#   date -u -d '+30 days' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ     # GNU date
#   date -u -v+30d +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ            # BSD/macOS date
#
# Being a top-level field it answers to BLNK_WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_SUNSET_DATE as well,
# the prefixed form winning if both are set.
WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_SUNSET_DATE=

# Legacy webhook destination policy (SSRF-01)
# The legacy HTTP transport requires https and refuses to deliver to an address inside
# Blnk's own network. That is not a check on the URL you typed: the address is judged
# immediately before the socket is opened, which is the only place a hostname that
# resolves to 127.0.0.1 is visible, and every redirect is refused outright because a
# 307 or 308 from a subscriber endpoint would repost your signed ledger payload
# wherever it pointed.
#
# Set this to true ONLY when the destination is on a network you own — an on-premise
# receiver behind RFC1918, or a loopback sink in development. It opens exactly two
# things: http, and delivery to loopback and private/ULA addresses.
#
# It is NOT an off switch, and it cannot be used as one. With it set to true these
# stay refused: every redirect, the link-local range including the 169.254.169.254
# cloud metadata endpoint, the unspecified address, multicast, the carrier-grade NAT
# range (100.64.0.0/10, where the GKE metadata proxy lives), the benchmarking and
# IETF protocol-assignment ranges, an internal IPv4 address smuggled through the
# NAT64 well-known prefix, and every non-HTTP scheme. Enabling it logs a warning
# naming what it permitted, so the assertion is visible in the deployment's log.
#
# Default false. Leave it unset in production unless you deliver internally.
#BLNK_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE_DESTINATION=true
BLNK_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE_DESTINATION=false

# Event relay retry
# Bounded exponential backoff for the outbox-to-Kafka relay, mirroring
# config.RelayConfig field for field - all three are R-10 CONTRACT VARIABLES with the
# defaults the requirement fixes - and read from the process environment or the
# matching keys under "relay" in blnk.json. The relay makes at most
# RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS attempts, waits RELAY_RETRY_BASE_BACKOFF_MS before the
# first retry and doubles thereafter, and never sleeps longer than
# RELAY_RETRY_MAX_BACKOFF_MS. Both delays are milliseconds and are used verbatim.
#
# The defaults below give five publish attempts separated by FOUR waits — 1s, 2s, 4s
# and 8s, 15s of backoff in total. The 30s cap is deliberately out of reach: the delay
# after a fifth failure would be 16s and there is no sixth attempt, so the cap only
# engages if you raise the base delay or the attempt count. Every attempt is logged with its number and error reason, and on
# exhaustion the event is written to its category's <topic>.dlt with failure metadata
# attached.
#
# These names are spelled bare for the same reason as the Kafka block's, and the same
# three forms and precedence apply: BLNK_RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS wins, then the bare
# RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS below, then BLNK_RELAY_RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS.
RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=5
RELAY_RETRY_BASE_BACKOFF_MS=1000
RELAY_RETRY_MAX_BACKOFF_MS=30000

# Event outbox retention (days)
# How long a DELIVERED or DEAD-LETTERED event row is kept before it is deleted. This is
# a data-protection control, not a storage tuning knob: every row's payload is the
# webhook body verbatim, so a transaction event carries amounts and balance identifiers
# and an identity event carries names, email addresses, phone numbers, postal addresses
# and dates of birth. None of it has operational value once the event is delivered, so
# keeping it forever makes this table an unbounded second copy of the ledger's most
# sensitive data — without the access controls the primary tables have around them.
#
# Only TERMINAL rows are ever eligible. A pending, processing, replaying or failed row
# is still owed a delivery attempt and is never deleted however old it is; a failed row
# in particular is excluded because its dead-letter write is still owed, which makes the
# outbox the only copy of that event in existence.
#
# 0 (the default, and the value below) DISABLES retention. That is deliberate: deleting
# ledger-adjacent records is a decision only you can take, because the period your
# jurisdiction, audit programme or any legal hold requires is not something a default
# can guess. Set a positive number of days to switch it on — 30 and 90 are common
# choices — and the sweep then runs hourly in the server role, deleting in bounded
# batches so it never blocks the relay. Rows removed are counted by
# blnk.events.purged.total.
RELAY_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=0

# Local Kafka stack
# Consumed by docker-compose interpolation for the local single-broker KRaft service and
# by scripts/kafka-provision.sh, following the POSTGRES_IMAGE / POSTGRES_CONTAINER /
# POSTGRES_OUTER_PORT precedent above. BLNK READS NONE OF THE KEYS IN THIS SECTION - they
# configure the local broker and its provisioning, not the application - so none of them
# is part of the R-10 contract described at the top of the Kafka block. The topic geometry
# the broker is provisioned with comes from KAFKA_MIN_PARTITIONS and
# KAFKA_REPLICATION_FACTOR above, which the application and the provisioning script share
# rather than duplicate.
#
# These services only start when the "kafka" compose profile is active - see the note
# on KAFKA_BROKERS above for what activates it.
#
# The image tag has a floor at BOTH ends, and neither is a preference.
#
# LOWER FLOOR - functionality. SASL bootstrap runs "kafka-storage format
# --add-scram", which requires Kafka 3.5 or later (Confluent Platform 7.5.0 or
# later). In KRaft mode SCRAM credentials live in the metadata log and at least one
# user must exist before brokers can authenticate at all, so an earlier image cannot
# be bootstrapped and local bring-up fails outright.
#
# LOWER FLOOR - security. Three advisories bound the choice. They do not share one
# floor and one of them has no patched release at all, so each is stated exactly
# rather than collapsed into "take the newest tag".
#
# NO "SAFE UPGRADE TARGET" IS NAMED HERE, deliberately. A version recorded in a comment
# as the safe one is correct on the day it is written and wrong from the next advisory
# onward, and a stale recommendation is read as current: this note previously pointed at a
# 4.0.x release that a later advisory superseded. What is durable is the functional floor
# above, the tested pin below, and where to look - https://kafka.apache.org/cve-list.
#
# 3.9.x rather than 4.x is a scope choice, not a claim about which is safer: this stack
# needs only SCRAM, and staying on the line it is tested against avoids re-validating the
# KRaft formatting, the SCRAM bootstrap and ACL enforcement. Any move off the pin means
# re-running those three checks, because they are what the stack depends on and what a
# major version can change.
KAFKA_IMAGE=apache/kafka:3.9.2
KAFKA_CONTAINER=kafka


# Where a GENERATED sample subscriber password is delivered, on the same terms as
# KAFKA_SASL_SECRET_FILE above: mode 0600, path reported, value never printed. With
# neither this nor the secret above set, the provisioning script refuses to run rather
# than print a credential into the kafka-init container log.
#KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/blnk-kafka-sample-subscriber
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET_FILE=

# KAFKA_OUTER_PORT is the HOST port the broker's external listener is published on,
# and it is the only one of the two that was ever configurable. The in-container port
# is fixed at 9092 by the listener configuration in docker-compose.yaml, where
# BROKER://kafka:9092 is what every other container dials; a KAFKA_INNER_PORT variable
# used to be listed here and was read by nothing, so setting it changed no port and
# silently promised something the stack does not do.
KAFKA_OUTER_PORT=9092

# KAFKA IS OPT-IN. The kafka and kafka-init services sit behind the "kafka" Compose
# profile, so "docker compose up" starts neither — which is what lets the documented
# no-broker steady state above actually start. Selecting the profile is what brings up
# the broker and provisions the topic catalogue and the two principals.
#
#   docker compose up                          -> no Kafka at all
#   docker compose --profile kafka up           -> broker + provisioning
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka  (set below)         -> the same, without the flag
#
# OPTING IN HAS TWO HALVES AND YOU NEED BOTH: select the profile so there is a broker,
# and set KAFKA_BROKERS=kafka:9092 so Blnk publishes to it. The profile alone gives a
# provisioned broker Blnk ignores; KAFKA_BROKERS alone gives a relay retrying against
# nothing. Add "monitoring" alongside it — COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka,monitoring — to bring
# up Prometheus and the two Kafka alerts as well.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka
COMPOSE_PROFILES=
# The host interface the broker's SASL listener is published on. LEAVE IT AT LOOPBACK.
#
# That listener speaks SASL_PLAINTEXT, so the SCRAM exchange and every ledger event on
# it travel unencrypted. Published on 0.0.0.0 - which is what compose does when no host
# address is given - it is reachable from anywhere that can route to this machine, which
# puts financial amounts and identity records on the wire in cleartext for anyone on the
# path AND exposes the broker superuser handshake. Everything that uses this port is
# local: host-run tests, a host-run "blnk start", and the k6 event scenario. Change it
# only together with a plan for encrypting the listener.
KAFKA_OUTER_HOST=127.0.0.1


# Advanced local-broker knobs, listed because the compose files interpolate them and an
# operator cannot otherwise discover that they exist. Every one carries the default shown, so
# leaving them commented changes nothing; they are here to be found, not to be set.
#
# KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL selects the listener the broker publishes and the protocol every
# client speaks to it. SASL_PLAINTEXT is authenticated but NOT encrypted, which is why the
# application additionally requires KAFKA_INSECURE_LOCAL_DEV to be acknowledged above. Use
# SASL_SSL with KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=true for anything reachable beyond this machine.
#KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=SASL_PLAINTEXT

# KAFKA_SCRAM_ITERATIONS is the PBKDF2 iteration count every SCRAM credential this stack
# creates is derived with. Kafka's floor is 4096 and both scripts refuse anything lower.
# Raising it costs a little CPU per authentication and nothing else.
#KAFKA_SCRAM_ITERATIONS=4096

# KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID pins the KRaft cluster ID the broker's storage is formatted with. Unset
# means one is generated on the first format and then read back from the volume's own
# meta.properties on every later start. Set it only to reproduce a specific cluster identity;
# a value that disagrees with an already-formatted volume makes formatting fail outright.
#KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID=

# The sample subscriber scripts/kafka-provision.sh creates so a local consumer has an
# identity to use. It is granted Read and Describe on the three subscriber-grantable category
# topics and Read on its own consumer-group prefix - never Write, never a .dlt topic, never
# an internal category. Leave the secret empty and one is generated on the first run, written
# to a mode-0600 file and preserved on later runs so a routine bring-up cannot invalidate the
# credential a consumer is already holding. Set KAFKA_SKIP_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER=1 on a shared or
# production broker, where subscriber principals belong to
# POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials rather than to a script.
#KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_USER=blnk-sample-subscriber
#KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET=
#KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_GROUP_PREFIX=blnk-sample-subscriber

KAFKA_OUTER_ADVERTISED_HOST=localhost


# Prometheus scrape authentication
# Blnk's /metrics endpoint is guarded by MetricsAuthHandler, and its behaviour is what
# decides whether the two Kafka alerts in alerts/blnk-kafka-alerts.yml can ever fire.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET for the local monitoring profile and scrapes succeed
# unauthenticated, which is why prometheus.yml ships with its authorization blocks
# commented out.
#
# SET IT and every request without "Authorization: Bearer <token>" is refused — which
# means the scrape jobs collect NOTHING, every series the alert rules match on is
# absent, and both alerts sit permanently unable to fire. That failure is silent:
# Prometheus reports the target as down, the rules report no error, and an alert that
# cannot fire is indistinguishable from a system with nothing wrong. So if you set
# this, follow the two enabling steps documented in prometheus.yml — the token file
# mount plus uncommenting the authorization blocks — and then confirm at
# http://localhost:9090 that /targets shows both jobs UP *and* /rules lists
# DeadLetterMessageStuck and SubscriberConsumerLagHigh.
#
# One token guards both the server and the worker endpoints. In Kubernetes it is not
# optional: infrastructure/k8s-manifests/prometheus-configmap.yaml authenticates
# unconditionally and reads the value from Secret blnk-metrics-token.
#
# BLNK_METRICS_BEARER_TOKEN=
